# Navigation Transitions

For interactive buttons that trigger navigation via `router.push()`, provide instant visual feedback while the route change is pending.

## Required pattern

1. Use [useTransitionRouter](mdc:remote-v2-frontend/src/hooks/useTransitionRouter.ts) instead of calling `useRouter().push()` directly from click handlers.
2. Bind `isPending` from the hook to the triggering button's `isLoading` prop ([Button](mdc:remote-v2-frontend/src/components/ui/button.tsx) supports `isLoading`).
3. Keep `router.push()` inside the hook's `push` / `replace` helpers so navigation runs inside `startTransition`.

```tsx
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button";
import { useTransitionRouter } from "@/hooks/useTransitionRouter";

const Example = () => {
  const { push, isPending } = useTransitionRouter();

  return (
    <Button isLoading={isPending} onClick={() => push("/dashboard/employees")}>
      Continue
    </Button>
  );
};
```

## Lists and menus

When multiple buttons can navigate, track which item was clicked and scope loading to that control:

```tsx
const [activeId, setActiveId] = useState<string | null>(null);
const { push, isPending } = useTransitionRouter();

<Button
  isLoading={isPending && activeId === item.id}
  onClick={() => {
    setActiveId(item.id);
    push(`/dashboard/orders/${item.id}`);
  }}
>
  View
</Button>
```

## Non-button triggers

If navigation is started from a tab, menu item, or custom control without `isLoading`, disable the control while `isPending` is true.

## Do not

- Call `router.push()` directly on `Button` / `ActionItem` click handlers for user-initiated navigation.
- Show a full-page loader for these transitions when a button-level `isLoading` state is enough.
